WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.334
A sustainable CMMC program begins with governance.

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:08.417
The organization needs accountable leaders, defined roles, approved policy, decision authority,

00:00:08.417 --> 00:00:13.961
issue escalation, supplier expectations, and a way to track changing contract obligations.

00:00:13.961 --> 00:00:18.120
Contract analysis and information discovery come before tool selection.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:23.664
Teams should understand applicable clauses, required CMMC status, FCI and CUI categories,

00:00:23.664 --> 00:00:27.823
data flows, recipients, locations, external services, and subcontractor relationships.

00:00:27.823 --> 00:00:30.595
A clear system boundary reduces uncertainty.

00:00:30.595 --> 00:00:33.829
Inventories, diagrams, ownership, approved applications, security-protection assets,

00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:39.836
and external providers help the organization explain which environments are covered and why.

00:00:39.836 --> 00:00:43.532
Gap analysis compares current implementation with applicable requirements.

00:00:43.532 --> 00:00:47.691
Findings need accountable owners, realistic milestones, resources, risk visibility,

00:00:47.691 --> 00:00:48.615
and verification.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:54.159
A policy statement alone does not resolve a technical or operational gap.

00:00:54.159 --> 00:00:56.008
Documentation should match reality.

00:00:56.008 --> 00:01:00.628
The System Security Plan, policies, procedures, inventories, diagrams, tickets, logs,

00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:01.552
training records,

00:01:01.552 --> 00:01:07.559
and supplier records should describe and demonstrate the way the environment actually operates.

00:01:07.559 --> 00:01:10.793
Supplier governance is part of the program.

00:01:10.793 --> 00:01:14.027
Organizations need processes to identify flow-down requirements,

00:01:14.027 --> 00:01:17.724
communicate handling expectations, review provider responsibilities, track status,

00:01:17.724 --> 00:01:22.806
and avoid sharing protected information before approved safeguards are in place.

00:01:22.806 --> 00:01:26.041
Continuous monitoring helps maintain the assessed condition.

00:01:26.041 --> 00:01:29.737
Access reviews, configuration checks, vulnerability management, incident response,

00:01:29.737 --> 00:01:32.047
change management, evidence review, training,

00:01:32.047 --> 00:01:37.592
and internal assessments can detect drift before it becomes a larger gap.

00:01:37.592 --> 00:01:39.440
Metrics should support decisions.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.060
Leaders need visibility into scope coverage, unresolved requirements, overdue remediation,

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.757
supplier risk, training completion, evidence health, exceptions, incidents,

00:01:47.757 --> 00:01:50.529
and changes that could affect status.

00:01:50.529 --> 00:01:57.460
The most effective programs treat CMMC as part of normal contract performance and cybersecurity operations.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.156
Controls become routine work, evidence is produced naturally,

00:02:01.156 --> 00:02:05.315
and compliance supports protection rather than competing with it.

00:02:05.315 --> 00:02:06.239
CMMC 2.

00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:11.783
0 helps the Department of Defense verify safeguards for FCI and CUI

00:02:11.783 --> 00:02:14.093
across the Defense Industrial Base.

00:02:14.093 --> 00:02:18.714
Success depends on current official requirements, disciplined implementation, honest evidence,

00:02:18.714 --> 00:02:20.100
and continuous improvement.
